entry.summary:
A celtic knot only looks right if the band goes over, under, over, under at every crossing. Most printable "knot" coasters cheat: the strands just merge into one flat blob where they cross. I wanted a trivet that reads as a real woven band, still prints flat with no supports, and stays one solid piece. Here is how I built it in about 50 lines of Python with manifold3d. A torus knot winds P times around the axis and Q times around the tube. Pressed flat, that gives a rosette with eight petals: P, Q = 3, 8 R0, A = 50.0, 35.0 # mean radius, petal depth (mm) N = 1200 # samples along the band
def path(): t = np.linspace(0, 2*math.pi, N, endpoint=False) r = R0 + A*np.cos(Q*t) a = P*t xy = np.c_[r*np.cos(a), r*np.sin(a)] return xy, np.sin(2*Q*t)
